<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mansura, Sindh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mansura was the capital of the Arab province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, founded in the 730s by the Umayyad governor Amr ibn Muhammad near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, north-east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. From the 850s it was the seat of the Habbari dynasty, and Arab geographers described it as a prosperous trading city. It declined after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>'s conquest in the 1020s, and its ruins lie near Shahdadpur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansura-sindh.jpg" alt="Mansura, Sindh" width="284" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wasimawan at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coins_Amirs_of_sindh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mansura, the triumphant city, was known in later centuries as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanabad">Brahmanabad</a> or Bahmanabad. It stood on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> amid fertile farmland, and historians describe it as a town of mango orchards and date-palm groves. Its site lies 18 kilometres south-east of Shahdadpur and 75 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. William Vincent identified the place with Patala, the city whose chief, Mæris, submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and then fled into the desert with his people, leaving the Macedonians an empty town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanabad">Brahmanabad</a> territory, the Umayyad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, al-Hakam, founded a city called al-Mahfuza at a time when, the sources say, no place in Sind was safe for Muslims, and his deputy al-Thaqafi founded al-Mansura opposite it. The historian Blankinship believes that one was a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamani">Yamani</a> troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and the other for the Mudari. By the time of al-Baladhuri the old Brahmanabad was in ruins and the new city carried its name. In the Abbasid era it was developed by Khalid ibn Barmak and grew into a metropolis richer than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debal">Debal</a>.</p><p>Ibn Hauqal described wealthy merchants of Sindhi-Arab origin dressed in the fashion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, living in houses of clay, baked brick and plaster. The city sent herbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, metals and mirrors to Baghdad, and its schools produced the first translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> into Sindhi. Its people included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> compiler Abu Mashar Sindhi. An earthquake struck Mansura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debal">Debal</a> in 893. When the inhabitants refused to open their gates to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>, his forces sacked the city, which never recovered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins lie on an open sandy plain, a boot-shaped field of brick mounds about 9.25 kilometres round, crossed by the hollows of old streets and ringed by the remains of walls and bastions. Nothing stands above the surface but one tower-like core of brick; there is no stone masonry, and charred wood shows where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> once was. The largest ruin is the courtyard of a Jamia Masjid. In early excavations A. P. Bellasis found a seal inscribed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> al-Baqir, thought to belong to the fifth Shi'ite imam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mansura, Sindh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As Abbasid control weakened, Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Habbari, from an Arab family settled in Sindh, made himself ruler around 854 at <b>al-Mansura</b>, the provincial capital near modern Shahdadpur, while continuing to recognise the caliph.</blockquote><a href="/state/habbari-dynasty">Habbari dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
